  nodeTextLayers:
  - title: 'What does an insulin monomer do?



'
    basic: Insulin monomers signal the body to reduce sugar in the bloodstream, thus
      protecting tissues from damage by excessive sugar.
    intermediate: 
    advanced: "Insulin plays a key role in regulating blood glucose homeostasis.
      \nIt signals to lower the overall glucose level in the body by various means.
      \nFor example, insulin promotes glucose storage in the liver and muscle cells
      by stimulating glycogenesis. \nInsulin also increases glucose uptake in adipose
      cells to enhance lipogenesis. \nInsulin also inhibits catabolic processes that
      generates glucose in skeletal muscles."
  nodeSpriteLayer: []
